## 2. Discovery — config-manifest-driven

### [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY] OPEN — discovery is config-manifest-driven, not package/entry_points auto-discovery

- question: how does the loader learn WHICH plugin classes to instantiate for a tenant?
- decision: (architect-proposed, flagged for David's ratify) a per-tenant MANIFEST declares which plugins to load, each entry `{plugin: <import-path-or-registered-name>, config: <typed dict>}`. The loader imports/resolves the class, validates `config` against that class's pydantic `Config` model BEFORE instantiation, instantiates on success, and hands the instances to the existing `standup()`/`ComposeServer.add_tenant()` binder unchanged.
- rationale: this fits the ratified pydantic-declaration + config-over-bus model already governing every other plugin surface ([CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]'s one-typed-declaration-derives-three-artifacts shape) — the manifest is the SAME kind of typed, validated declaration applied one level up, at "which plugins does this tenant run" rather than "what does one plugin's config look like". It requires no new discovery machinery beyond an importer and a dict-to-pydantic validation call, both already idioms this codebase uses (`transport_registry.py`'s `type:` factory is the direct precedent — [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-CONTRACT] already lifted that same idiom to the connector layer; this node lifts it again to the plugin layer).
- rejected_alternative: (not chosen for v1, recorded so it is not silently lost) `entry_points`/setuptools package auto-discovery — scanning installed packages for a registered plugin group. Rejected for v1 because it couples plugin availability to what happens to be PIP-INSTALLED in the current environment rather than to what the TENANT'S OWN MANIFEST declares, which is a weaker per-tenant boundary than [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] wants: two tenants sharing one Python environment would see the SAME auto-discovered set unless a second, separate filter were added on top — the manifest already gives that filter for free as its primary purpose, not a bolt-on.
- alternatives: entry_points/setuptools package auto-discovery (REJECTED for v1, see `rejected_alternative` above).
- invalidating_assumption: if a deployment later needs a plugin's mere PRESENCE on the Python path to be sufficient for it to load — no manifest edit required, e.g. a plugin-as-a-pip-package ecosystem with many third-party authors — entry_points auto-discovery becomes worth the coupling cost and should be added as a SECOND discovery mode alongside the manifest, not a replacement for it. (Restated as a top-level property so the DD-family schema can find it directly, mirroring [DD-CBK-PRIMARY-RENDER-SURFACE]'s precedent; the fuller record lives in `context_for_change` below.)
- change: "discovery is config-manifest-driven; entry_points/package auto-discovery is NOT v1."
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: "no discovery mechanism exists at all — every current call site (tests, any bring-up script) hand-constructs plugin instances and passes them directly to `standup(plugins=[...])`."
  reasoning: "a manifest is the smallest addition that turns hand-wired instances into a declared, per-tenant, validated set, and it reuses the plugin contract's own typed-declaration idiom rather than inventing a second one."
  rejected_alternative: "entry_points/package auto-discovery (see rejected_alternative above)."
  invalidating_assumption: "if a deployment later needs a plugin's mere PRESENCE on the Python path to be sufficient for it to load — no manifest edit required, e.g. a plugin-as-a-pip-package ecosystem with many third-party authors — entry_points auto-discovery becomes worth the coupling cost and should be added as a SECOND discovery mode alongside the manifest, not a replacement for it (a manifest entry would still be required to CONFIGURE a discovered class, since config validation has no other input)."
  implication: "the loader interface ([CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE]) takes a manifest dict as its primary input; no scanning of `sys.path` or `importlib.metadata.entry_points()` is implemented in this pass."
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-proposed
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE], [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-CONTRACT]
- settled-by: DECIDED 2026-11-26 by [CXC-PC-DISCOVERY] in 95-plugin-contract.lts ([SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]): the host's install records are the registry. Entry-point scanning and package-import discovery are NOT adopted (entry points require install before consent and carry no metadata). The tenant manifest names an installed plug-in by `domain`; dotted import paths remain for built-in default plug-ins only. (Architect, on the shared plug-in contract.)

### [CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE] The manifest entry schema — `{plugin, config}`, resolved by registered name or dotted import path

- claim: a tenant manifest is `{"plugins": [<entry>, ...]}`. Each `<entry>` is `{"plugin": <ref>, "config": <dict>}`, where `config` is OPTIONAL (defaults to `{}`, which validates against a `Config` whose every field has a default — the same rule `ComposePlugin._coerce_config(None)` already applies for a hand-instantiated plugin with `config=None`).
- ref-resolution: `<ref>` resolves to a `ComposePlugin` subclass in ONE of two ways, tried in this order: (1) a REGISTERED NAME — `<ref>` is a key in the caller-supplied `registry: dict[str, type[ComposePlugin]]` (e.g. `"time"` -> `TimePlugin`); (2) a DOTTED IMPORT PATH — `<ref>` contains a `:` splitting `"module.path:ClassName"`, imported via `importlib.import_module` then `getattr`. A `<ref>` with no `:` that is ALSO absent from `registry` is a resolution failure (see [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY]), never silently treated as a bare module import.
- resolved-class-must-subclass: a resolved object that is not a `ComposePlugin` subclass is a resolution failure, never instantiated — a manifest cannot be used to construct an arbitrary class.
- open-question-registry-source: NOT resolved by this spec — whether ComposeServer ships one canonical registered-name -> class registry (e.g. covering the default-loaded plugins `time`/`liveness`/`log`/`state_manager`/`discovery`, [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGINS]/[CXC-CS-EXTRACTION-INVENTORY]) that every caller reuses, or whether every caller always supplies its own `registry` dict. [CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE] makes `registry` an explicit parameter (default `None` = dotted-path-only resolution) so the loader is buildable either way; the canonical-registry question is left for the architect's own follow-up, not blocking this spec.
- realizes-note: this node REALIZES [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY] — the edge is authored explicitly in `## Relationships` rather than duplicated here as a second, non-minting inline property.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify (carries [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY]'s open status)
- provenance: architect-proposed
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY], [CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE]

---

## 3. Config validation — before instantiation, park loudly on failure

### [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION] MUST validate `config` against the plugin's pydantic `Config` BEFORE instantiation; invalid config PARKS the plugin loudly

- requirement: (MUST, testable) for each manifest entry whose class resolves successfully, the loader MUST validate `entry.get("config", {})` against the resolved class's `Config` model by constructing `cls.Config(**config)` DIRECTLY — never by constructing `cls(config=...)` first and inspecting whether it raised. This makes "before instantiation" a property of the CALL SEQUENCE a builder can verify by reading the code, not an incidental consequence of where inside `ComposePlugin.__init__` pydantic happens to raise.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a `pydantic.ValidationError` raised by that `cls.Config(**config)` call MUST be caught and MUST NOT propagate out of the loader, MUST NOT construct the plugin instance, and MUST NOT prevent any OTHER manifest entry in the same tenant from being resolved/validated/instantiated. One malformed plugin entry never takes down the loader run or a sibling plugin.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) on a config validation failure the loader MUST record a park entry keyed by the plugin's declared `name` (if the class resolved and exposes a `name` ClassVar) or, if resolution itself failed, keyed by the raw manifest `<ref>` string — with a reason string per invalid field, per [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY]'s `config:` taxonomy. The reason MUST name the specific field and the pydantic message, never a generic "config invalid" string — mirroring [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]'s admin-UX-names-the-unmet-item rule, applied to config instead of capabilities.
- negative-control: a manifest entry whose `config` fails validation is NEVER instantiated (no `ComposePlugin.__init__` call reaches its body for that entry) and NEVER appears in the list handed to `ComposeServer.add_tenant(plugins=...)` — a test asserts the constructed-instances list excludes it while every valid sibling entry in the same manifest IS instantiated.
- on-success: the ALREADY-VALIDATED `Config` instance is passed to `cls(config=validated)` — `ComposePlugin._coerce_config` accepts a `cls.Config` instance verbatim (`isinstance(config, cls.Config)` short-circuits its own re-validation), so the plugin is never double-validated.
- verify-machine: a test manifest with (a) one entry whose config is valid, (b) one entry whose config fails one field's type validation, (c) one entry whose config fails two fields — asserts (a) is instantiated and started, (b) and (c) are parked (never instantiated) with EXACTLY the failing field count worth of `config:` reasons each, and (a)'s instantiation is unaffected by (b)/(c)'s failure (ordering-independent: parking (b)/(c) first or last in the manifest list produces the same result for (a)).
- verify-human: none required — this node's failure surface (a raised exception vs. a caught, named park) is fully machine-observable; there is no glass-only signal here.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY], [CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE], [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]

