## 4. The `requires:` execution floor — reused, not respecified

### [CXC-CSL-REQUIRES-FLOOR] MUST evaluate each plugin's `requires:` floor and PARK unmet plugins loudly, naming the specific unmet item

- requirement: (MUST, testable) for every successfully instantiated plugin, the loader MUST evaluate its `requires:` floor via the EXISTING `ComposePlugin.unmet_requirements(available_capabilities, ready_evaluator)` — this spec does not reimplement that evaluation; it is realized by handing the instantiated plugin list to the EXISTING `ComposeServer.add_tenant(..., plugins=<instances>, capabilities=<tenant caps>)`, which already calls `standup()`, which already calls `unmet_requirements()` per plugin ([CXC-CS-STANDUP-OWNERSHIP], landed).
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a plugin whose floor is unmet MUST be recorded PARKED with the exact `unmet` list `unmet_requirements()` returns — each item already prefixed `capability:<name>` or `ready:<expr>` (no evaluator) / `ready:<expr>` (evaluator returned false) by the LANDED code (`python/composeio/plugin.py::unmet_requirements`) — the loader passes this through verbatim; it does not paraphrase or collapse it to a generic message.
- negative-control: a plugin declaring `requires.capabilities = ["foo"]` with `available_capabilities` NOT containing `"foo"` is parked with `unmet == ["capability:foo"]` and is NOT started (`plugin.start(bus)` is never called for it) and does NOT appear in `LoaderResult.standup.loaded`.
- verify-machine: a manifest with one plugin `requires.capabilities=["missing-cap"]` loaded against a tenant with an empty capability set asserts the plugin is parked with `unmet == ["capability:missing-cap"]`, is absent from `loaded`, and its `start()` was never invoked (a spy/mock on `start`).
- verify-human: none required for this node specifically (the mechanism is entirely reused, already-landed code); see [CXC-CSL-CONTINUOUS-REBIND] for the verify.human covering the full park-then-unpark arc across a real capability arrival.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — the `requires:`-floor semantics (park-loud on an unmet floor, naming the specific unmet item, never silently degraded) are David's ruling, recorded at [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR] (`park-loud-not-silent`, `admin-UX-names-the-unmet-item`, line 23288). This node's contribution is realizing that already-david-ruled floor for the LOADER's own call path — the mechanics of wiring it through are architect-synthesis, the semantics enforced are David's.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR], [CXC-CSL-CONTINUOUS-REBIND]

### [CXC-CSL-CONTINUOUS-REBIND] MUST re-evaluate continuously via the existing binder — a parked plugin unparks with NO restart

- requirement: (MUST, testable) the loader MUST NOT implement its own re-evaluation loop or polling mechanism. Continuous re-evaluation is realized ENTIRELY by handing the tenant off to the EXISTING `ComposeServer` binder (`provide_capability()`/`_reevaluate()`, landed in `python/composeio/composeserver.py`) — once `load_tenant_manifest()` has called `server.add_tenant(...)`, any subsequent `server.provide_capability(tenant_id, cap)` call against that SAME `ComposeServer` instance re-evaluates every parked plugin's floor and starts any now-eligible one, with no restart of the tenant's bus or of any already-running plugin.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a plugin that was PARKED for a config-validation failure (never instantiated, [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION]) does NOT unpark via `provide_capability()` — a config-invalid plugin has no `requires:` floor to re-evaluate because it was never instantiated; it can only be recovered by a NEW manifest load with a corrected `config:`. This is a stated boundary, not a silent gap: `provide_capability()`'s re-evaluation loop iterates `tenant.plugin_specs`, which only ever contains successfully-instantiated plugins.
- negative-control: a plugin parked for `capability:X` stays parked (absent from `loaded`, present in `parked`) across an UNRELATED `provide_capability(tenant_id, "Y")` call (`Y != X`), and only unparks on `provide_capability(tenant_id, "X")` — proving the re-evaluation is capability-specific, not a blanket "try everything again".
- verify-machine: a test loads a manifest with one plugin `requires.capabilities=["cap-x"]` against an empty capability set (asserts parked), then calls `server.provide_capability(tenant_id, "cap-x")` (asserts the SAME plugin instance — identity-checked, not merely name-checked — now appears in `tenant.plugins`/`loaded`, its `start()` was called exactly once, and the tenant's bus object is unchanged — no restart).
- verify-human: David (or composeio-first-bringup) drives a real capability arrival end-to-end against a real tenant bus — a plugin declared with `requires.capabilities=["some-real-capability"]` stays visibly parked on `_server/plugins/<name>/status` (glass: the admin surface reads `status: parked`, `unmet: ["capability:some-real-capability"]`) until the capability is actually announced on the bus, at which point the SAME running process transitions it to `status: running` with NO process restart observed (no new PID, no bus reconnect in the log). Machine-green here is compatible with a loader that only unparks in a narrow unit-test harness and never wires the real announce-driven capability signal — [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS] applies.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — realizes [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]'s `dynamic-rebinding` property (David's ruling that the floor is re-evaluated continuously, not once at load time, line 23288), applied to the loader's own call path. The `plugin_specs`-only-contains-instantiated-plugins boundary (config-invalid plugins cannot unpark) is architect-synthesis, following directly from [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR], [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION]

---

## 5. The subprocess-pending seam — reused, not respecified

### [CXC-CSL-SUBPROCESS-PENDING] MUST record a subprocess-floor plugin as `subprocess_pending` and NOT start it in-process

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a plugin whose `resolved_isolation(server_isolation_minimum) >= Isolation.SUBPROCESS` MUST be recorded in `LoaderResult.standup.subprocess_pending` and MUST NOT have `start(bus)` called on it in-process. This is realized entirely by the EXISTING `standup()` logic (`python/composeio/composeserver.py::standup`, the `if actual >= Isolation.SUBPROCESS: result.subprocess_pending.append(...); continue` branch) — the loader does not reimplement this check; it is inherited automatically because the loader hands its instantiated plugins to `standup()`/`add_tenant()` unchanged.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) the isolation check MUST run only on SUCCESSFULLY instantiated plugins (config-valid AND requires-floor-met is NOT a precondition — isolation resolution and requires-floor evaluation are independent checks both run by `standup()` per plugin, in the ORDER the landed code already fixes: `unmet_requirements()` first — an unmet floor parks before isolation is even checked — then, only for a floor-met plugin, the isolation check). The loader MUST NOT reorder this.
- negative-control: a plugin with `isolation_floor = Isolation.SUBPROCESS` and a FULLY MET `requires:` floor is still never started in-process — its name appears in `subprocess_pending`, `start()` is never called on it, and it is absent from `loaded`.
- verify-machine: a test plugin subclass declaring `isolation_floor = Isolation.SUBPROCESS` with an empty `requires:` floor (trivially met) is loaded via a manifest and asserted: present in `subprocess_pending`, absent from `loaded`, its `start()` mock never invoked.
- verify-human: a maker/architect reviewing a real Phase-0 bring-up log confirms a subprocess-pinned plugin is visibly reported `subprocess_pending` on `_server/status` and is NOT observed publishing on the bus (no announce beyond the loader's own derived one) — the IPC daughtercard host is Phase 1, so "correctly held pending, never silently started anyway" is the entire glass check.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — the subprocess-pending seam is David's subprocess-model ruling ([CXC-CBK-SUBPROCESS-DAUGHTERCARD], "a plugin whose resolved isolation is >= subprocess ... is recorded `subprocess_pending` and NOT started in-process; the IPC daughtercard host is Phase 1 — the seam is explicit"), and the no-force-inprocess/HARD-subprocess-pin halves of [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR] are also David's (line 23247; Q1 ruling 2026-08-23). This node records that the loader inherits this seam by construction (reusing `standup()` unchanged) rather than re-deriving it — that inheritance framing is architect-synthesis.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-SUBPROCESS-DAUGHTERCARD], [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-ENCODING], [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]

---

## 6. Multi-tenancy — strictly per-tenant, no shared instances

### [CXC-CSL-PER-TENANT] MUST be strictly per-tenant — each tenant's manifest loads its own plugin instances, never shared

- requirement: (MUST, testable) `load_tenant_manifest()` MUST instantiate a FRESH plugin object for every manifest entry it processes, for the tenant it is called with. It MUST NOT cache or reuse a plugin instance across two different `tenant_id` values, even when two tenants' manifests declare the IDENTICAL `{plugin, config}` entry.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) loading the SAME manifest for two different tenants MUST produce two DISTINCT `ComposePlugin` instances (`is not`, not merely `==`) — this is [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]'s LOCKED-PERMANENTLY invariant, applied at the loader's own instantiation step, which is the exact point a caching optimization would otherwise be tempted to violate it.
- negative-control: calling `load_tenant_manifest(server, "tenant-a", manifest)` then `load_tenant_manifest(server, "tenant-b", manifest)` with the SAME manifest dict object produces `server.tenant_bus("tenant-a")` and `server.tenant_bus("tenant-b")` as distinct `ComposeIOBus` objects (already guaranteed by `ComposeServer.add_tenant`, landed) AND each tenant's plugin instance for the same manifest entry is a distinct object with independent `._bus` state — a test asserts `id(plugin_a) != id(plugin_b)` for the matching entry across the two tenants.
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided — the invariant is LOCKED; this node records the loader's specific obligation not to break it
- provenance: david-ruling — [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] is LOCKED PERMANENTLY: "plugins are ALWAYS per-tenant; NO shared plugin instances, ever" (David's ruling on [DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]). This node is the loader-specific corollary; the corollary framing is architect-synthesis.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]

---

## 7. Status surface — extending the already-built announce path to loader-only park reasons

### [CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE] SHOULD surface load/park state — including config/resolve parks the existing binder never sees — on `_server/plugins/<name>/status` and the `_server/status` aggregate

- requirement: (SHOULD, testable) every plugin `standup()` already tracks (loaded / requires-floor-parked / subprocess_pending) is ALREADY published by the LANDED `publish_server_state()`/`publish_plugin_state()` ([CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE], built) once the loader hands its instances to `ComposeServer.add_tenant()` — no new work is needed for those three states.
- the-gap-this-node-closes: a plugin that PARKED FOR CONFIG VALIDATION OR RESOLUTION FAILURE ([CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION]) never reaches `standup()` at all — it was never instantiated, so it cannot appear in `publish_plugin_state()`'s per-plugin loop (which calls `plugin.announce()`, an INSTANCE method requiring a constructed plugin). Left alone, a config-invalid entry would be invisible on the admin surface — a silent drop, exactly what [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]'s park-loud discipline exists to prevent, now at one remove.
- requirement: (SHOULD, testable) the loader SHOULD publish a `_server/plugins/<key>/status` entry directly (via `bus.publish_state`, the SAME namespace/payload shape `publish_plugin_state()` already uses for a status: `{"status": "parked", "unmet": [...]}`) for every config/resolve-parked entry — `<key>` is the resolved class's `name` if resolution succeeded (validation failed only), or the raw manifest `<ref>` string if resolution itself failed. This entry carries NO `_server/plugins/<key>/announce` (there is no valid instance to derive one from) — a status-only entry is the honest representation of "we know this was declared and it failed before we could learn anything else about it."
- requirement: (SHOULD, testable) the loader SHOULD merge config/resolve parks into the tenant's `_server/status` aggregate `parked` dict alongside the requires-floor parks the binder already tracks, so a single query against `_server/status` sees every reason a tenant's manifest is not fully running — config-invalid, resolution-failed, and requires-floor-unmet are three rows of the SAME `parked` mapping, not three surfaces an operator must separately check.
- verify-machine: a test loads a manifest with one config-invalid entry and asserts `_server/plugins/<ref-or-name>/status` was published with `status: "parked"` and a `config:`-prefixed `unmet` list, AND that the tenant's `_server/status.parked` dict contains that same key.
- verify-human: an operator viewing a real `_server/status` payload for a tenant with a deliberately-malformed manifest entry confirms the malformed entry is NAMED and its failure reason is READABLE (not a stack trace, not silence) — the admin surface must read as "this specific plugin, this specific reason" the same way [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]'s park messaging already does for capability parks.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — extends [CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE] (decided, architect-synthesis, already built) to cover a park class that mechanism did not anticipate because it predates this spec's config-validation park.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE], [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION]

