## 8. Error taxonomy — extending the existing `unmet` prefix convention

### [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY] Park-reason strings extend the LANDED `capability:`/`ready:` prefix convention with `config:` and `resolve:`

- claim: `ComposePlugin.unmet_requirements()` (landed) already prefixes every unmet-floor reason by class: `capability:<name>` (a missing capability) and `ready:<expr>` (an unmet or unevaluated readiness predicate). This spec extends the SAME convention with two loader-native prefixes rather than inventing an unrelated format:
  - `resolve:<message>` — the manifest `<ref>` could not be resolved to a `ComposePlugin` subclass (unknown registry key with no `:` in the ref; `ImportError`/`AttributeError` on a dotted path; a resolved object that is not a `ComposePlugin` subclass). ONE `resolve:` item per failed entry — resolution either succeeds or it does not, there is no partial resolution to enumerate.
  - `config:<field>: <message>` — ONE item per pydantic `ValidationError` error dict entry (`err["loc"]`/`err["msg"]`), so a config with three invalid fields produces three `config:` items, mirroring `capabilities` producing one `capability:` item per missing capability.
- why-extend-not-invent: a consumer of `_server/plugins/<name>/status` (a human reading the admin UX, or a future automated dashboard) already has to parse `capability:`/`ready:` prefixes; adding `resolve:`/`config:` to the SAME small closed set costs that consumer nothing new to learn, versus a second unrelated error-shape the consumer would need a second parser for.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION], [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]

---

## 9. The loader interface — what composeio-expert builds

### [CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE] The concrete function signatures — `resolve_plugin_class`, `load_tenant_manifest`, `LoaderResult`

- module: a NEW module, `composeio/composeserver_loader.py` — separate from `composeio/composeserver.py` (which this spec deliberately does not modify) and `composeio/plugin.py` (the frozen contract, also unmodified). The loader is a CONSUMER of both, not an addition to either.
- resolve-plugin-class: `resolve_plugin_class(ref, registry=None) -> type[ComposePlugin]`, where `ref` is `str` and `registry` is `dict[str, type[ComposePlugin]] | None`. Resolves `ref` per [CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE]'s two-form rule (registered name in `registry`, else a `"module.path:ClassName"` dotted import). Raises `PluginResolutionError(ref, reason)` — a NAMED exception, never a bare `ImportError`/`KeyError` propagating past the loader — on any failure, so the caller in `load_tenant_manifest` can catch exactly this type and convert it into a `resolve:` park per [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY].
- load-tenant-manifest: `load_tenant_manifest(server, tenant_id, manifest, *, registry=None, connectors=None, capabilities=None, start=False) -> LoaderResult`, where `server` is `ComposeServer`, `tenant_id` is `str`, `manifest` is `dict`, `registry` is `dict[str, type[ComposePlugin]] | None`, `connectors` is `list[dict] | None`, `capabilities` is `set[str] | None`, and `start` is `bool`. The entry point. For each entry in `manifest.get("plugins", [])`: (1) `resolve_plugin_class` — on failure, record a `resolve:` park keyed by the raw `ref` and continue to the next entry; (2) `cls.Config(**entry.get("config", {}))` — on `pydantic.ValidationError`, record `config:` parks keyed by `cls.name` and continue; (3) on success, `cls(config=validated)`, appended to this tenant's `plugins` list. After all entries are processed, call the EXISTING `server.add_tenant(tenant_id, connectors=connectors, plugins=plugins, capabilities=capabilities, start=start)` UNCHANGED, and merge its `StandupResult.parked` with the loader's own `config_parked` dict for the `_server/status` publish per [CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE].
- `LoaderResult` (a new dataclass, mirroring `StandupResult`'s inspectability): `tenant_id: str`; `standup: StandupResult` (the object `add_tenant()` returns — requires-floor `parked`, `loaded`, `subprocess_pending`, all UNCHANGED semantics); `config_parked: dict[str, list[str]]` (manifest-ref-or-name -> `resolve:`/`config:` reasons, [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY]); `is_clean() -> bool` returning `self.standup.is_clean() and not self.config_parked`.
- reuse-by-reference: (what this module does NOT reimplement) `standup()`'s three-step sequence, the `requires:`-floor evaluation, the isolation/subprocess-pending check, the multi-tenancy binder (`provide_capability`/`_reevaluate`), and the `_server/plugins/<name>/announce`+`status` publish for every INSTANTIATED plugin — all consumed verbatim from `composeio/composeserver.py` and `composeio/plugin.py` as they exist on `origin/main` today. This spec's entire net-new surface is: manifest parsing, class resolution, pre-instantiation config validation, and the config/resolve-park status publish gap they open up.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION], [CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE], [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY], [CXC-CS-STANDUP-OWNERSHIP]

---

## 10. Acceptance — machine signal + glass gate

### [CXC-CSL-ACCEPTANCE] The loader's two-tier verdict — a machine signal AND, for every failure class that can silently swallow a plugin, an explicit human/glass gate

- definition: per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE] and the render-engine precedent ([CXC-DVL-*] `verify.machine`/`verify.human`), machine-green alone never accepts this loader — the failure classes that matter most here (a silently-dropped malformed plugin, a plugin stuck parked forever, a subprocess-floor plugin quietly started in-process anyway) are exactly the ones a narrow unit test can pass while the real thing is broken. [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS] applies throughout this table.

| ID | Requirement | verify.machine | verify.human (glass) |
|----|-------------|-----------------|------------------------|
| CSL-V1 | Invalid config PARKS, never crashes ([CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION]) | a manifest mixing valid/invalid entries: valid entries instantiate+start, invalid entries park with per-field `config:` reasons, no exception escapes the loader call | none required — fully machine-observable |
| CSL-V2 | Unresolvable `ref` PARKS, never crashes ([CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE], [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY]) | a manifest entry with an unknown registered name AND one with a broken dotted import path both park with a `resolve:` reason naming the ref; sibling valid entries are unaffected | none required |
| CSL-V3 | Unmet `requires:` floor PARKS, naming the unmet item ([CXC-CSL-REQUIRES-FLOOR]) | a plugin requiring an absent capability parks with `unmet == ["capability:<name>"]`, absent from `loaded` | an operator reads the SAME reason on `_server/plugins/<name>/status`, not a generic failure |
| CSL-V4 | Continuous rebind — parked-for-X stays parked until X arrives, then runs, no restart ([CXC-CSL-CONTINUOUS-REBIND]) | negative control: an unrelated `provide_capability(Y)` leaves an X-parked plugin parked; `provide_capability(X)` starts the SAME instance, no bus restart | David/first-bringup drives a real capability arrival end-to-end and confirms the transition on `_server/status` with no process restart observed |
| CSL-V5 | Subprocess-floor plugin never starts in-process ([CXC-CSL-SUBPROCESS-PENDING]) | a `SUBPROCESS`-floor plugin with a MET `requires:` floor still lands in `subprocess_pending`, never `loaded`, `start()` never called | a real Phase-0 bring-up log/`_server/status` shows the plugin held `subprocess_pending`, never silently running in-process |
| CSL-V6 | Strictly per-tenant, no shared instances ([CXC-CSL-PER-TENANT]) | loading the identical manifest for two tenant ids yields two distinct plugin object identities and two distinct buses | n/a — structural, fully machine-observable |
| CSL-V7 | Config/resolve parks surface on the admin surface, not silently dropped ([CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE]) | `_server/plugins/<key>/status` and `_server/status.parked` both carry the config/resolve-parked entry | an operator confirms the malformed entry is NAMED and its reason is human-readable on the real admin surface, not merely present in a log line only a developer would think to grep |
| CSL-V8 | Placement recorded, not silent ([CXC-CSL-PLACEMENT]) | the spec carries the `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` node + the filename-check property | n/a — structural |

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE], [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS]

