## 12. Open forks — flagged for the architect's / David's ratify

### [DD-CSL-MANIFEST-FORMAT] OPEN — the manifest FILE format and per-tenant location are unspecified

- question: [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY]/[CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE] specify the manifest's ABSTRACT shape (`{"plugins": [{"plugin", "config"}, ...]}`) but not its concrete FILE serialization (YAML? JSON? a `.lts` fragment, consistent with config-over-bus?) or WHERE per tenant it lives (a fixed path convention like `<tenant-root>/plugins.yaml`? a bus-published config value, per [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]'s config-over-bus model, so a manifest change is itself a bus-native operation rather than a file edit?).
- why-left-open: `load_tenant_manifest()` ([CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE]) takes an already-PARSED `manifest: dict` as its parameter — the function signature does not require this question to be answered to be implementable and testable; only the (unspecified) code path that PRODUCES that dict from a file/bus-value needs it. Leaving it open does not block building or acceptance-testing the loader's own logic against a hand-constructed dict.
- candidates: (not a recommendation, offered so the fork is concrete rather than abstract) (a) YAML file at a fixed per-tenant path; (b) a bus-published config value under `_server/config/plugins` per the config-over-bus precedent ([SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]), giving manifest changes the SAME reactive/observable shape every other config value already has on this platform; (c) an `.lts` fragment, consistent with this corpus's own self-hosting property ([PROFILE-SELFHOST]) but an unusual choice for a RUNTIME artifact rather than a design document.
- decision: OPEN — not yet decided. Three candidates are offered above (YAML file / bus-published config value / `.lts` fragment); none is chosen. `load_tenant_manifest()` ([CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE]) is buildable and testable against a hand-constructed `dict` regardless of which candidate is later ratified.
- rationale: see `why-left-open` above — the loader's own function signature does not require this question answered to be implementable; deferring costs nothing to this spec's own acceptance criteria.
- alternatives: (a) YAML file at a fixed per-tenant path; (b) bus-published config value under `_server/config/plugins`; (c) an `.lts` fragment — see `candidates` above for the tradeoffs of each.
- invalidating_assumption: once a real deployment needs to WRITE a manifest (not just read one at standup), the choice stops being deferrable — a bus-published value implies a write API this spec does not specify, and a file implies a deployment/ops convention this spec does not specify either.
- status: open
- status-detail: open — flagged for architect/David ratify
- provenance: architect-proposed (the question is named in the dispatch that produced this spec; the candidate list is this spec-writer's own addition, offered to make the fork concrete, not a recommendation)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY], [CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE]
- partially-settled-by: PARTIALLY DECIDED 2026-11-26 by [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FILE] in 95-plugin-contract.lts (its r22 decision node CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FORMAT-DECISION moved in spec 95 r23 to (a private draft, in the private companion), section "CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FORMAT-DECISION"): the PACKAGE manifest is `manifest.json` at the plug-in package root (HA's file name and format). STILL OPEN here: the serialization and per-tenant location of this spec's TENANT manifest (`{plugin, config}` entries).

### [DD-CSL-HOT-RELOAD] OPEN — manifest hot-reload; architect's lean is OUT of v1

- question: if a tenant's manifest changes at runtime (a plugin entry added or removed), does the loader detect and apply that change without a process restart?
- lean: (architect-proposed, not decided) OUT of v1. A PLUGIN re-announces its own surface changes ([CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE], already built and reused unchanged by this spec) — but ADDING or REMOVING a plugin FROM THE MANIFEST at runtime is a different, larger operation (constructing/tearing down a whole plugin instance, not merely re-announcing an existing one's surface) and is deferred rather than folded into this pass.
- why-deferrable: [CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE]'s `load_tenant_manifest()` is a ONE-SHOT call per tenant at standup time; nothing in this spec's MUST requirements depends on it being re-callable against a running tenant with a changed manifest. Deferring costs nothing to THIS spec's acceptance criteria.
- decision: OPEN — architect leans OUT of v1 (see `lean` above); not yet ratified by David.
- rationale: see `why-deferrable` above — nothing in this spec's MUST requirements needs manifest hot-reload, so deferring it costs this spec nothing.
- alternatives: IN v1 — the loader gains a re-callable `reload_tenant_manifest()` (or similar) that diffs a new manifest against `tenant.plugin_specs`, constructing/tearing down instances for added/removed entries. Not designed here; would be its own follow-up spec if ratified.
- invalidating_assumption: if a real deployment needs to add/remove a plugin without a process restart before v1 ships, this lean reverses and hot-reload becomes in-scope.
- status: open
- status-detail: open — flagged for architect/David ratify (leaning OUT of v1, not decided)
- provenance: architect-proposed — the dispatch names this exact lean ("my lean: OUT of v1 — a plugin re-announces its own surface, but adding/removing a plugin from the manifest at runtime is deferred").
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE], [CXC-CSL-LOADER-INTERFACE]

